Let me write a playwright eval script that iterates classes and returns skill names/costs. Use `page.evaluate` via `playwright-cli eval`. The `eval` CLI may accept a function string. Let me do:

```
() => {
  const out = {};
  const select = document.getElementById('classSelect');
  for (const cls of ['warrior','mage','cleric','paladin']) {
    select.value = cls;
    select.dispatchEvent(new Event('change', {bubbles:true}));
    out[cls] = [...document.querySelectorAll('.spell-slot[data-skill]')].map(b => ({name: b.querySelector('.spell-name').textContent, cost: b.querySelector('.spell-cost').textContent, disabled: b.disabled}));
  }
  return out;
}
```

Then check AO_DEBUG fighters' skills? AO_DEBUG doesn't expose skills. But the UI is enough. Also test healing value: switch to cleric, set player hp low via debug? No setter. Could wait for damage, then cast heal. Complex. Maybe use the game's own simulation: run for 20s and check floating texts include "Sanar" and + values within range. The `AO_DEBUG().texts` captures floating texts. We can monitor over 30s for "Sanar", "Fuente de vida", "Ráfaga Ígnea", "Apocalipsis", "Juicio Final", "Castigo Divino". Also check no errors. Let's do that.

Also verify damage numbers? The floating texts include `-117` etc. We can collect texts and check values. Let me write a probe that samples AO_DEBUG().texts every 100ms for ~30s and aggregates spell names and damage values. Note floating texts live ~1.05s; sampling every 100ms catches them.

